  "eos_positions": [],
  "content": "# Binary Search and `lower_bound`\n\n## The Big Idea: Binary Search\n\nImagine you're looking up a name in a **physical phone book**. You wouldn't start at page 1 and flip one page at a time. Instead, you'd open it roughly in the middle, see whether the name you want comes before or after the current page, and then **throw away half the book**. You repeat this until you find your name.\n\nThat's binary search: **repeatedly halving the search space** by comparing your target to the middle element.\n\nBut there's one crucial rule: **binary search only works on a sorted list.** If the data isn't sorted, \"throw away the half that can't contain it\" doesn't make sense.\n\n### Why is it so fast?\n\nEach step cuts the remaining candidates in half:\n\n```\n1024 items \u2192 512 \u2192 256 \u2192 128 \u2192 64 \u2192 32 \u2192 16 \u2192 8 \u2192 4 \u2192 2 \u2192 1\n```\n\nThat's only **11 steps** to search 1024 items. For a billion items, it takes about **30 steps**. This is",
